      "math_academy_id": "q-342713",
      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$",
